<title>PORTUGAL: No escudo devaluation needed for EMU -IGCP chief.</title>
<headline>No escudo devaluation needed for EMU -IGCP chief.</headline>
<dateline>LISBON 1997-02-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Portugal's entry into monetary union in 1999 would not require any devaluation of the escudo in the run-up to the single currency, the state's debt manager said.</p>
<p>&quot;Nobody can convince me that the escudo is in any way overvalued,&quot; Vitor Bento, chairman of the Institute for Public Credit Management (IGCP) told Reuters in an interview.</p>
<p>&quot;This notion (the need for a depreciation of the escudo to enter the euro) has no scientific basis,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Bento said that the economy was functioning well (with the current rate of the escudo), there were no balance of payments difficulties and exports were gaining market share.</p>
<p>&quot;I believe (the current) level is sustainable,&quot; Bento added.</p>
<p>--Lisbon bureau 3511-3150035</p>
</text>
